 # 1Password CLI
 
+Retrieve secrets from 1Password vaults — don't create or store them (that's human territory).
+
+## When to Use
+
+- **Inject secrets into commands**: `op run -- ./deploy.sh` (secrets as env vars)
+- **Template injection**: `op inject -i template.env -o .env` (fill placeholders)
+- **Retrieve specific secrets**: When a script or tool needs credentials
+- **List vault contents**: See what's available (without exposing values)
+
+## When NOT to Use
+
+- **One-time entry**: If user can just type the password, don't automate it
+- **Already in env vars**: Check `env | grep -i api` first — might already be set
+- **Creating secrets**: Humans should create/store secrets in the 1Password app, not via CLI
+- **Desktop app not running**: `op` requires the 1Password desktop app to be open and unlocked
+- **Non-sensitive data**: Regular config files don't need vault storage
+- **Temporary/test credentials**: Overhead not worth it for throwaway values
+
+## Overlap Clarification
+
+| Task | Use This | Why |
+|------|----------|-----|
+| "Run deploy with API key from 1Password" | **op** ✓ | `op run` injects secrets |
+| "I need to enter my password" | User types it | One-time, interactive |
+| "Save this new API key" | User saves in app | Humans manage secret storage |
+| "Script needs DATABASE_URL" | Check env first | Might already be set |
+| "Need AWS creds for boto3" | **op** ✓ | Inject via `op run` |
+
+## Security Rules
+
+- **Never log secrets**: Don't echo, print, or include in command output
+- **Prefer `op run`/`op inject`**: Secrets stay in memory, never touch disk
+- **Don't hardcode secret references**: Use templates or env vars
+- **Clean up**: If you must write a secret file, delete it immediately after use
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- 1Password desktop app installed, running, and unlocked
+- `op` CLI installed and integrated with desktop app
+- App integration enabled in 1Password settings
+
+---
+
 Follow the official CLI get-started steps. Don't guess install commands.
 
