 # Coding Agent (bash-first)
 
+Spawn a dedicated coding AI for sustained development work — not for simple edits you can do yourself.
+
+## When to Use
+
+- **Feature development**: Building something that needs multiple files, tests, iteration
+- **PR code review**: Agent can read the diff and provide detailed analysis
+- **Multi-file refactoring**: Renaming, restructuring, updating patterns across codebase
+- **Bug investigation**: Agent can explore code, run tests, trace issues
+- **Parallel tasks**: Spin up multiple agents for independent issues/features
+- **Full-auto mode**: Let agent build something end-to-end with minimal intervention
+
+## When NOT to Use
+
+- **Simple edits**: Use `edit` tool yourself — faster and no agent overhead
+- **Reading code**: Just `read` the file — you can understand it
+- **Non-code decisions**: If user wants YOUR analysis or opinion, don't delegate
+- **One-liner fixes**: `edit` tool, not a whole agent
+- **Config/docs**: Usually simpler to do directly
+- **Already know the solution**: If you know exactly what to change, just change it
+
+## Overlap Clarification
+
+| Task | Use This | Why |
+|------|----------|-----|
+| "Add error handling to all API calls" | **coding-agent** ✓ | Multi-file, needs exploration |
+| "Fix typo in README" | `edit` tool | One line, trivial |
+| "Build a REST API for todos" | **coding-agent** ✓ | Feature build |
+| "What does this function do?" | Read it yourself | Just comprehension |
+| "Review PR #55" | **coding-agent** ✓ | Needs code analysis |
+| "Change port from 3000 to 8080" | `edit` tool | One change |
+
+## Key Rule
+
+**Don't delegate thinking.** If the task requires judgment, analysis, or decision-making, that's your job. Coding agents are for execution, not strategy.
+
+---
+
 Use **bash** (with optional background mode) for all coding agent work. Simple and effective.
 
